 "narration": "Two lawyers paid one dollar to bottle Coca-Cola. And a bottle of Coke stayed at five cents for 70 years. 1886. Coca-Cola goes on sale for a nickel. Then come wars, then the Great Depression. The price doesn't move. 1899. Two Chattanooga lawyers, Benjamin Thomas and Joseph Whitehead, ask Coke's boss Asa Candler for the right to bottle it. Candler says yes, for one dollar. He also sells them syrup at a fixed price. Forever. Coke paints the price everywhere. Signs on the sides of stores read: Drink Coca-Cola, five cents. Then come vending machines. Most take just one coin: a nickel. A dime would double the price, a 100 percent jump. Too big. But then, Coke's boss Robert Woodruff asks his hunting buddy, President Eisenhower, for a new coin: seven and a half cents. The Treasury doesn't like it. No new coin. Costs keep climbing. Prices start to creep: six cents, seven, ten. 1959. The last nickel Coke disappears, after more than 70 years. A one dollar deal, and 70 years at a nickel. Would you promise one price forever?",
 "seed": 3306
